  <h2>Method and how to cite</h2>
  <p>
    The index counts tools sold for one job: a reviewer reporting on a live webpage. Screen-video tools and design-file
    commenting are out. Jam records a bug instead of pinning a comment and is counted because it is sold for that job. For each tool I recorded three things from the vendor's own site: whether a free plan is
    permanent, the list price of the cheapest paid plan on monthly and on annual billing, and what the vendor says a
    reviewer needs. Promotions are ignored. Where a page contradicts itself or leaves a field out, the table says so
    instead of guessing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Third-party figures are included only where the original publication could be opened and the number found in it. Most
    are surveys run by companies that sell to the people they surveyed, so read them with that in mind. Corrections are welcome through the contact on the <a href="/about">about page</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    To cite a first-party figure, credit the {INDEX} and link to <a href={path}>{ORIGIN}{path}</a>. The page keeps this
    address each year and the index is re-read when prices move.
  </p>

